## Context

Ticket 1365 (PR #2044) found that codex review seats inherited
`model_reasoning_effort = "low"` from `~/.codex/config.toml` (set 2026-10-29
for interactive use, on padme and doudou alike), and gpt-6-luna then refused
4 of 56 launches without reading the tree. The fix pinned one key on the
door (`-c 'model_reasoning_effort="medium"'` in `skills/route/routes.json`);
every other key of the host file (provider, reasoning summaries, profiles,
MCP servers, anything added later) still reaches every seat unseen. The same
holds for the other headless doors (claude, pi, vibe) to the extent each CLI
reads a user config.

The author: "I hate hidden settings, plus arena". A seat's behaviour should
be what `routes.json` declares (a règlement, readable in the repository),
not what a host file happens to say; the arena bench needs the same for its
arms to be comparable across hosts and days.

`codex exec` offers `--ignore-user-config` (and `-p/--profile` layering a
`$CODEX_HOME/<name>.config.toml`). Whether login credentials still load with
`--ignore-user-config` is unchecked.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] Each headless seat door in `skills/route/routes.json` launches without reading the host's user CLI config, or the door records why its CLI cannot (and which keys it then pins)
- [ ] Every setting a seat needs (model, effort, sandbox, tools) is declared on the door; a test shows a seat launch is unchanged when the host user config sets a conflicting value (for codex: effort low in a fake user config)
- [ ] One real launch per changed door reads its review tree (login still works)

Arena counterpart: filed in the arena repository (its legs may bind the host
`~/.codex/config.toml` in some modes, `runner.py` LEG_CREDENTIALS).

## Raid plan (raid 2b69a0, 2026-10-30)

Imagine, per door (`skills/route/routes.json`):
- codex: add `--ignore-user-config` (help: skips `$CODEX_HOME/config.toml`, auth still from `CODEX_HOME`). Keep the `-c` effort pin. Confirm with `codex debug prompt-input` (no model call), with and without the flag, that the `-c` pin and sandbox still apply. The flag also drops the host's codex hooks (`[hooks.state]`) and trust entries in seats: record that in the door.
- claude: NOT sealed. `--safe-mode` still loads user settings; `~/.claude/settings.json` sets `effortLevel` low and per-model overrides, and the door pins `--model` but no effort. Prefer `--setting-sources project` (or none) plus an explicit `--effort`; `--bare` is out (breaks subscription login). Definite change, so a real launch is required.
- pi: no skip flag; `PI_CODING_AGENT_DIR` would move auth. Pin thinking level explicitly (refuse a pi model given without `:<thinking>`), record the remaining limit (default provider from host settings) on the door.
- vibe (disposable `VIBE_HOME`) and aider (explicit flags, `--env-file /dev/null`): already isolated, no change.
No new abstraction: `build_command` already fills templates; no guard scanning doors.

First test: `tests/test_gaze_seat.py`: a door built from the real `routes.json` carries the isolation flags; plus a codex shim under the `rig` fixture with a fake user config setting effort low, showing the argv the seat runs is unchanged. Real launch per changed door (codex, claude, pi if reachable on padme; otherwise say unreachable) reading its review tree.

Feasibility: PASS. Shares `tests/test_gaze_seat.py` with 1370 (disjoint regions).
